---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ORGS, STALE_DAYS } from '../data/orgs.ts';
import { REPORT_ISSUE_BASE } from '../data/site.ts';

/**
 * The "recent changes" feed is built from each record's change_log. It is
 * empty until the Phase 4 update agent runs; showing the section anyway, with
 * an honest empty state, is better than hiding how the site is maintained.
 */
const changes = ORGS.flatMap((org) =>
  org.change_log.map((c) => ({ org, change: c })),
)
  .sort((a, b) => b.change.date.localeCompare(a.change.date))
  .slice(0, 40);

const counts = {
  total: ORGS.length,
  high: ORGS.filter((o) => o.confidence === 'High').length,
  medium: ORGS.filter((o) => o.confidence === 'Medium').length,
  low: ORGS.filter((o) => o.confidence === 'Low').length,
  flagged: ORGS.filter((o) => o.status !== 'active').length,
};
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="How this directory is put together, what the confidence levels mean, and how it is kept current."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>About this directory</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A free, non-commercial directory of animal help in New York City. No ads, no tracking,
      no accounts. It exists because the information people need in a crisis is scattered across
      dozens of sites, and half of it is out of date.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in it</h2>
    <p>
      {counts.total} organizations: rescues, shelters, TNR groups, 24-hour emergency vets,
      low-cost clinics, wildlife rehabilitators, trap banks, hotlines and owner-support
      programmes, across all five boroughs. It covers cats, dogs, rabbits, birds, reptiles,
      small mammals, farm animals and wildlife.
    </p>

    <h2 id="confidence">What the confidence levels mean</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><strong>High</strong> ({counts.high} entries)</dt>
      <dd>The contact details were confirmed on the organization's own website on the date shown.</dd>
      <dt><strong>Medium</strong> ({counts.medium} entries)</dt>
      <dd>
        Confirmed through an authoritative listing — the city shelter's partner roster, a state
        licence register, a well-maintained referral directory — or the entry carries a caveat,
        such as being based outside the city.
      </dd>
      <dt><strong>Low</strong> ({counts.low} entries)</dt>
      <dd>
        A real group, but reachable mainly through a social media account or a third-party
        directory. These are worth knowing about, and worth checking before you rely on them.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      Confidence is about <em>evidence</em>, not quality. A small, excellent, one-person rescue
      that only uses Instagram is Low confidence; that is a statement about how firmly we could
      verify its contact details, not about the rescue.
    </p>

    <h2>Whether a group is still operating</h2>
    <p>
      That is tracked separately from confidence, because they are different problems. A
      well-verified group that has since shut down is more dangerous to a person in a hurry than
      a thinly-verified group that is thriving. {counts.flagged} entries are currently flagged as
      closed, paused, moved out of the city, or needing confirmation, and each says so on its card.
    </p>

    <h2>Why entries say "confirm before relying on this"</h2>
    <p>
      Rescue contact details go stale fast. Volunteers move on, small groups fold quietly, and
      phone numbers get reassigned. Any entry that is Low confidence, flagged as not currently
      active, or that has not been verified in the last {STALE_DAYS} days carries that reminder.
      It is not a warning about the organization — it is a reminder that this directory is a
      starting point, not a guarantee.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Some of the research behind this site contained private individuals' phone numbers —
      a volunteer's mobile, a home line for a rehabilitator working out of their house. Those are
      withheld by default until the person confirms they want to be listed publicly. Where that
      leaves an entry without a direct contact, the entry says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site sets no cookies, runs no analytics, and asks for nothing about you.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is kept current</h2>
    <p>
      An automated check will run every week against each organization's own website, looking for
      changed phone numbers and emails, dead domains, and language like "we have closed" or "not
      currently accepting intakes". It is deliberately cautious:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A contact detail is only changed automatically when the new value appears on the organization's own site <em>and</em> the old one no longer does.</li>
      <li>Nothing is ever deleted automatically. Entries that fail repeatedly are flagged for a person to look at.</li>
      <li>Anything ambiguous is flagged rather than quietly overwritten.</li>
      <li>If a single run would change more than a small fraction of the directory, it changes nothing — that pattern means the checker broke, not that half of New York's rescues moved.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every change is recorded, and shown below.</p>

    <h2>Recent changes</h2>
    {changes.length === 0 ? (
      <p class="muted">
        No automated changes yet — the weekly check has not run. Everything currently in the
        directory came from the initial research, verified in August 2026.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <ul>
        {changes.map(({ org, change }) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{change.date}</strong> — <a href={`/org/${org.id}`}>{org.name}</a>:
            {' '}{change.field} changed{change.from && <> from <code>{change.from}</code></>}
            {change.to && <> to <code>{change.to}</code></>}
            {change.evidence_url && <> (<a href={change.evidence_url} rel="noopener nofollow">evidence</a>)</>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )}

    <h2 id="report">Report a problem</h2>
    <p>
      If an entry is wrong, out of date, or should not be here, say so. Every organization page
      has a "report it" link that opens a pre-filled issue with the entry already identified.
      You can also <a href={REPORT_ISSUE_BASE} rel="noopener">open one directly</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:.92rem">
      Reports go to a public issue tracker, which means a report is visible to anyone. That is the
      trade-off for running this with no server, no database and no cost — there is nowhere
      private to put a message. Do not include anything you would not want public.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and takedowns</h2>
    <p>
      If you run one of these organizations and want your entry changed or removed, open an issue
      and it will be done. Nothing here is scraped from anywhere that asked not to be.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
